            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="1" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="1" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall your house is relatively air tight, however there are a few air leakage locations that can be addressed:



** The 6" chimney for the water heater is connected to the rest of the house through an opening into the crawlspace through the floor of the water heater room. The crawlspace is fully connected to the upper floors via the Hi-Velocity Heating system ductwork. So ........ when the wind blows outside and sucks heat up the chimney, that heated air is being pulled from throughout the house. The heating &amp; hot water system upgrade recommendation would eliminate that chimney.



** The ceiling trim above the rear entry door.



Savings: With your house being very air tight already, it is tough to achieve a significant reduction. I did model a small 10% reduction in air leakage, that is estimated to potentially save $ 102 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.1 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem />
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a combination system - an oil-fired domestic hot water heater that provides both domestic hot water &amp; space heating, coupled with the Hi-Velocity heat exchange/distribution unit. As mentioned, there is some corrosion present at one of the connections at the top of the tank &amp; you should have this tank reviewed for possible pending failure.



The current combination system is modeled with a heating system performance of 72.0% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), while the hot water side performs at an efficiency of about 0.56 Energy Factor (EF). I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler with a minimum heating efficiency of 95% AFUE, and a hot water performance of 0.90 EF. The current Hi-Velocity heat exchanger does not need to be changed.



Savings: By replacing your current combination boiler with a new ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane combination boiler (at the efficiency levels noted above), you could potentially save $ 970 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.3 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation:

        - the kitchen range hood fan

        - a bathroom fan

        - the clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing a timer switch connected to bathroom fan (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so the fan can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (range hood fan, both bathroom fans and the dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 7.0 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil-fired hot water tank does vent up a chimney and could be susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2018 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.18 per litre 

Propane - $2.42/m3 community piped system, $0.79 per litre delivered

Wood - $289 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $4.73 per bag, $256.46 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and ation costs, and climate change!gh transportation costs, and climate change!</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
